      "id": "t000172",
      "source": "Editorial Review of the Romanian Draft and Corrections in This English Edition",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000173",
      "source": "The Romanian draft did reach the numbering 1–1000, but it relied too heavily on repeated family templates and sometimes varied only names or numerical values. This English edition keeps the intended curriculum coverage while repairing the main weakness: every problem instance is generated from a complete data model, the answer is recomputed from that model, and the learner is required to identify the logical operation that justifies the result. The second half broadens both knowledge and reasoning with scale, coordinates, Earth–Sun relations, conservation, density, spatial compatibility, risk, ecosystems, sustainability, migration, temporal intervals, genealogy, anachronism, provenance, uncertainty, counterfactuals, public goods, commons, fairness, coalitions, due process, sampling, matching, and meta-reasoning.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000174",
      "source": "GRADE 1",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000175",
      "source": "Difficulty increases through the number of relations and reasoning steps, not through hidden knowledge.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000176",
      "source": "Part A — Foundational reasoning about the world",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000177",
      "source": "G1. Positions and orientation",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000178",
      "source": "Problem 1. Position chain from Cedar to Alder",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000179",
      "source": "Knowledge context.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000180",
      "source": "A map can be modeled with relative positions. When several moves are given, their effects can be combined rather than memorized as a picture.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000181",
      "source": "Given facts.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000182",
      "source": "Pine is 1 map unit(s) east of Cedar; Alder is 1 map unit(s) east of Pine.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000183",
      "source": "Rules.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000184",
      "source": "Treat east as +x, west as −x, north as +y, and south as −y. Add the moves in order. A fact that does not affect any coordinate is irrelevant.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000185",
      "source": "Task.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000186",
      "source": "Where is Alder relative to Cedar? What is the straight grid displacement measured as |x|+|y|?",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000187",
      "source": "Step-by-step solution.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000188",
      "source": "1. Start Cedar at (0,0). 2. Translate each relation into a coordinate move. 3. Adding the moves gives (2,0). 4. That coordinate is east of Cedar; the grid displacement is |2|+|0|=2.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000189",
      "source": "Answer.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000190",
      "source": "Alder is east of Cedar; grid displacement 2 unit(s).",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000191",
      "source": "Knowledge takeaway.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000192",
      "source": "Cardinal directions become easier to reason about when they are treated as relations that can be composed.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000193",
      "source": "Formal model.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000194",
      "source": "Grid coordinates; each statement adds a vector; sum the vectors and classify the final coordinate.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000195",
      "source": "Problem 2. Position chain from Iris to Pine",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000196",
      "source": "Knowledge context.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000197",
      "source": "A map can be modeled with relative positions. When several moves are given, their effects can be combined rather than memorized as a picture.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000198",
      "source": "Given facts.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t000199",
      "source": "Fern is 1 map unit(s) north of Iris; Pine is 1 map unit(s) north of Fern.",
      "translation": ""
